
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SUBJECT (the line above): what this commit does, in the imperative.
#
#   Finish the sentence "If applied, this commit will ___".
#     Remember the operator's audio input between launches
#     Stop blaming the microphone permission for every silent input
#     Fix a viewer leak on mid-send disconnect
#
#   Not "Fixed", not "Fixes", not "Changes to audio". Around 50 characters,
#   no full stop. If you need "and" twice, it is probably two commits.
#
# BODY (below the blank line): WHY. This is the half people skip, and the
# half that is worth something a year from now.
#
#   The diff already says what changed. It cannot say what you knew at the
#   time: what was broken and how you noticed, what you tried that did not
#   work, what you deliberately did NOT do and why. Someone running
#   `git blame` on a strange-looking line wants to know whether it was a
#   decision or an accident.
#
#   Worth writing down:
#     - the symptom, and how it showed up
#     - the cause, once you found it
#     - anything you rejected, and the reason
#     - how you checked it actually works
#
# BEFORE COMMITTING: run the tests. Every commit should be one you could
#   ship from.  .venv/bin/python tools/smoke_test.py "$(pwd)"
#
# Wrap the body at ~72 characters. Lines starting with # are ignored.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
